// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
//
// Lets argond schedule and cancel a poweroff when the UPS battery is critical.
//
// The Debian package installs this to /usr/share/polkit-1/rules.d/, which is where a
// package's rules belong. Installing by hand, use /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/.
//
// Overriding it is not simply a matter of dropping a file in /etc: polkit processes rules
// files in lexical order of BASENAME across both directories, and the first rule to return a
// non-undefined result wins (polkit(8)). /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/90-local.rules therefore runs
// AFTER this file and cannot override it. To override, reuse this basename in
// /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/ -- where it does take precedence for the same name -- or pick one
// that sorts earlier, e.g. 40-argon-local.rules.
//
// Why a rule is needed: argond runs as the unprivileged "argon" system user, outside any
// login session. On Debian 13 polkit lets an active session power off without asking, but
// requires administrator authentication for anything else (checked with
// `pkaction --verbose --action-id org.freedesktop.login1.power-off`: "implicit any:
// auth_admin_keep"). A daemon cannot answer an authentication prompt.
//
// Deliberately NOT granted: org.freedesktop.login1.power-off-ignore-inhibit. If a program
// holds a shutdown inhibitor, argond respects it rather than overriding it. The trade-off is
// real: an inhibitor held while the battery runs out means the UPS cuts power without a clean
// shutdown. Overriding other programs' inhibitors is a decision for the operator to make
// explicitly, not a default.
//
// Which actions are involved was verified on Debian 13 (polkit 126) with `pkaction
// --verbose`: scheduling with other users' sessions present checks
// power-off-multiple-sessions, scheduling with none checks power-off, and set-wall-message is
// covered by power-off's `imply` annotation. Both grants are load-bearing, because polkit
// implication is not documented as transitive.
//
// STILL UNTESTED as the "argon" user. T12 does NOT cover this: it ran argond inside a desktop
// session, where no rule is needed (see its capture record). The first real test of this file
// is the packaged service scheduling a poweroff -- task T13.

polkit.addRule(function (action, subject) {
    if (subject.user === "argon" &&
        (action.id === "org.freedesktop.login1.power-off" ||
         action.id === "org.freedesktop.login1.power-off-multiple-sessions")) {
        return polkit.Result.YES;
    }
});
